        },
        "notes": "Where the star's surface was darker and brighter in TESS sector 32, worked out in this project from how its light rose and fell by 1.7% as it turned once in 1.01 days. The light curve is measured from the mission's images with lightkurve, and starry finds the map that reproduces it. The longitudes of the dark and bright regions are fixed by the data; their latitudes are not. The map is made at the tilt the page draws the star with, 57°.",
        "legendNote": "Darker: dimmer than the star's mean surface; white: brighter. Black line: 57° S; the star never shows us what lies below it."
